  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how different kinds of land-use change can alter vector-borne disease dynamics across a landscape. It links activities such as deforestation, agriculture, livestock production, urbanization, mining, and dam construction to changes in pathogen transmission risk, vector abundance, and contact between humans and vectors.\n\nThe visual suggests that deforestation can shift local microclimates, change predator–vector interactions, and increase human–vector contact, with transmission risk potentially going either up or down. Agricultural land and livestock areas are also shown as capable of increasing or decreasing both vector diversity and pathogen transmission risk, while livestock additionally affects blood meal availability for vectors.\n\nUrbanization is depicted as promoting anthropogenic vector proliferation, increasing human–vector contact and vector reproduction, and reducing predation pressure, implying conditions that often favor disease vectors. Mining is shown more simply as increasing both transmission risk and human–vector contact. Dams appear to facilitate vector reproduction, likely by creating suitable aquatic habitats.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that human-driven environmental modification does not affect disease risk in a single uniform way, but repeatedly reshapes habitats and interactions in ways that often favor vectors and transmission.",
